You are the Repository Branch Cleanup & Hygiene Agent for fengie/mhw-mods. Execute the cleanup now; do not merely recommend it.

CANONICAL RULE:
- main is the canonical integration branch.
- Temporary task branches exist only while work is active.
- Once completed work is integrated into main, pushed, and verified on origin/main, delete the temporary local branch and remote counterpart, then prune.

MISSION:
1) Locate the actual local checkout(s)/worktrees for fengie/mhw-mods under C:\Users\Xxkan. The Heaven Local Bridge relay checkout may be on the active heaven-bridge branch; do not delete or disrupt the currently checked-out bridge branch or its worker infrastructure.
2) In the appropriate development checkout, read and obey:
   AGENTS.md
   GLOBAL_GIT_DIRECTIVE.md
   NEXT-AGENT-START-HERE.md
   _AGENT_CONTEXT/CURRENT_REVISION.json
   _AGENT_CONTEXT/CONTINUITY_PROTOCOL.md
   _AGENT_CONTEXT/LEARNED_RULES.md
   _AGENT_CONTEXT/README_FIRST.md
   Repository state and current remote main override stale assumptions.
3) Before ANY deletion: git fetch --all --prune; establish exact origin/main SHA and default branch; enumerate local branches, remote branches, worktrees, and current branch(es). Check protected/retained/release branches from repo policy and available GitHub/gh metadata if accessible.
4) For every branch, determine whether work is:
   (1) already fully incorporated into origin/main;
   (2) superseded by newer work already on main;
   (3) duplicated by another integrated branch;
   (4) associated with an already-merged/completed task;
   (5) still active or contains unique commits/changes not on main.
   Do not decide by age or branch name alone. Use git merge-base, rev-list, cherry, patch-id/diffs as needed, and associated PR metadata when useful.
5) NEVER delete main/default branch, current checked-out branch, protected branches, active release branches, explicitly retained branches, the active heaven-bridge infrastructure branch/worktree, or any branch with unique/unproven active work.
6) For every branch proven safe: verify useful work is represented on origin/main, delete local branch if present, delete remote branch if present, prune stale refs, then verify deletion.
7) Preserve uncertain branches and explain precisely why.
8) Enforce the FUTURE BRANCH RULE in the repository governance if an appropriate existing governance file does not already state it. Do not duplicate policy text gratuitously. The lifecycle is:
task -> temporary branch if needed -> implement -> test -> sync latest main -> reconcile -> merge/fast-forward into main -> push main -> verify remote main -> delete temporary local branch -> delete temporary remote branch -> prune -> DONE.
A task is not done while its obsolete temporary branch remains. If governance already contains an equivalent rule, make no policy edit.
9) If governance is changed, follow the repo's own integration rules: test/validate as relevant, integrate to main, push, verify origin/main, and remove the temporary task branch used for the edit.
10) Do not destroy any unique work. On uncertainty, preserve.

Return a concrete FINAL REPORT with:
- exact origin/main SHA used as cleanup baseline;
- repository path/worktree used;
- local branches deleted;
- remote branches deleted;
- branches preserved for unique/active work;
- branches preserved because status could not be proven safely;
- stale refs pruned;
- PR/branch anomalies;
- any governance file changed and resulting main SHA;
- final exact origin/main SHA after any policy update;
- confirmation no known unique work was destroyed.
Include relevant command evidence / branch SHAs where useful.
